{"answer":"No. The parity inference is valid, but it contradicts—not supports—the lowest-terms hypothesis. Thus the displayed reasoning cannot prove that sqrt(2) is rational. Completed as a reductio, it proves that sqrt(2) is irrational: assuming a lowest-terms representation leads to both a and b being divisible by 2.","approaches":[{"description":"Use parity directly: an odd integer has odd square, so from a^2=2b^2, a is even; substituting a=2c then shows b is even.","id":"parity-reductio","outcome":"candidate","reason":"This is self-contained and yields a contradiction with the lowest-terms condition."},{"description":"Treat “a and b are both even” as evidence for the claimed rationality conclusion.","id":"claimed-direction","outcome":"rejected","reason":"Both numbers being even violates, rather than establishes, that a/b is in lowest terms."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"question_premise","basis_reference":"The question states: \"write sqrt(2)=a/b in lowest terms, square to get a^2=2b^2\".","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"lowest-terms-setup","kind":"assumption","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The claimed setup assumes integers a,b with b nonzero, sqrt(2)=a/b, and no common factor greater than 1.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"local_assumption","basis_reference":"Temporary negation of the intended irrationality conclusion, instantiated by the lowest-terms setup.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":["lowest-terms-conflict"],"id":"rationality-assumption","kind":"assumption","scope_step_ids":["square-equation","a-even","b-even","lowest-terms-conflict"],"statement":"Assume temporarily that integers a,b with b nonzero and no common factor greater than 1 satisfy sqrt(2)=a/b.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by squaring the equality in the temporary assumption.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["rationality-assumption"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"square-equation-claim","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Under the temporary assumption, a^2=2b^2.","step_ids":["square-equation"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived using the elementary fact proved in step a-even: an odd integer has an odd square.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["square-equation-claim"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"a-even-claim","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Under the temporary assumption, a is even.","step_ids":["a-even"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Substitution of a=2c into a^2=2b^2, followed by the same odd-square argument.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["a-even-claim","square-equation-claim"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"b-even-claim","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Under the temporary assumption, b is even.","step_ids":["b-even"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"The contradiction discharges the temporary rationality assumption.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["rationality-assumption","a-even-claim","b-even-claim"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"irrationality-conclusion","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"No lowest-terms integer representation sqrt(2)=a/b exists; therefore sqrt(2) is irrational.","step_ids":["lowest-terms-conflict"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"square-equation","justification":"From sqrt(2)=a/b with b nonzero, square both sides and multiply by b^2.","statement":"a^2=2b^2."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["square-equation"],"id":"a-even","justification":"The equation makes a^2 even. If a were odd, a=2k+1 for some integer k, and a^2=4k^2+4k+1 would be odd. Hence a is even.","statement":"There is an integer c such that a=2c."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["square-equation","a-even"],"id":"b-even","justification":"Substituting a=2c into a^2=2b^2 gives 4c^2=2b^2, hence b^2=2c^2. Thus b^2 is even. Applying the odd-square calculation from step a-even to b shows b is even.","statement":"There is an integer d such that b=2d."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["a-even","b-even"],"id":"lowest-terms-conflict","justification":"Steps a-even and b-even show that 2 divides both a and b, contradicting the temporary assumption that they have no common factor greater than 1. This closes the reductio subproof.","statement":"The temporary lowest-terms representation is impossible."}],"question_status":"refuted","tool_requests":[]}
